378ca97ad0e8461b1919d970", "bytes": 164048, "lines": 402, "events_no_deltas": [{"type": "system", "subtype": "init", "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "tools": ["StructuredOutput"], "mcp_servers": [], "permissionMode": "auto", "claude_code_version": "2.1.294", "output_style": "default", "init_sha256": "19a6ffcf0781096cd8fadcc901d1f698c81748761870f4f438dc6f07a56b3d9a"}, {"type": "system", "subtype": "status", "status": "requesting"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "message_start", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgPUteFqsLL52NYhQ6ypw", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4967, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2269, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4967}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "ttft_ms": 1949, "thinking_display": "updates"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 0, "content_block": {"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "thinking_display": "updates"}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgPUteFqsLL52NYhQ6ypw", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": {"длина": 27500, "sha256": "f852aaaeb8cd5d7456b06c102e321397507cb9cd947bea957d2b6e9d7ba830e6"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4967, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2269, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4967}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-25T19:10:23.105Z", "request_id": "req_011CgPUte1hxx2ze8VceFu7r", "thinking_duration_ms": 99251}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgPUteFqsLL52NYhQ6ypw", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I picked **candidate 0**. It orders exactly the forecast, shifted by each item's lead time: for item 0 it orders day t+4's forecast, for item 1 day t+2's. The other candidates are the same plan scaled by about 0.9, 1.1, 1.2 or 1.3, and candidate 2 also orders every other day.\n\n**Same for every plan.** The starting stock (26 of item 0, 40 of item 1) runs out within the first days, before the first orders arrive. The shortfall penalties in those days are identical for all candidates, so I left them out.\n\n**How I compared them.** The plans never react to actual demand, so stock on hand behaves like a random walk that resets to zero when stock runs out. Each day it moves by the amount delivered minus demand. Rewriting the profit with this in mind:\n- Item 0: profit ≈ 10·(units bought) − 21·(units left at the end or expired) − storage cost.\n- Item 1: profit ≈ 14·(units bought) − 25·(units left at the end or expired) − storage cost.\n\nI estimated the expected leftover with the formula for the maximum of a random walk with drift. Average demand is 1.046 × forecast. Daily spread is about 32 % of the forecast, which gives about 35 units over the whole period for item 0 and about 61 for item 1.\n\n**Item 0** (expected profit by scale factor):\n\n| Scale | Expected leftover | Storage | Profit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.9 | ~8 | ~60 | ~4640 |\n| 1.0 | ~18 | ~125 | ~4910 |\n| 1.1 | ~46 | ~260 | ~4730 |\n| 1.2 | ~91 | ~470 | ~4110 |\n\n**Item 1:**\n\n| Scale | Expected leftover | Profit |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.9 | ~13 | ~11720 |\n| 1.0 | ~31 | ~12440 |\n| 1.1 | ~81 | ~12160 |\n| 1.2 | ~162 | ~10900, before losses from expiry |\n\nAt a scale of 1.0, one extra unit still earns a little, about +1. By 1.1 it already loses about −6. The best scale is therefore about 1.02, so ordering the forecast is closer to it than any other candidate.\n\n**Why the others lose:**\n- **Candidates 1, 6, 7 (scale about 1.2–1.3):** leftovers and storage grow too much. Candidate 6 only cuts back to the forecast for the last few orders, which doesn't save it.\n- **Candidate 3:** item 0 is at the forecast, but item 1 is at about 1.2, which costs roughly 1500 on item 1.\n- **Candidate 4 (1.1):** about 450 less than candidate 0.\n- **Candidate 5 (0.9):** shortage penalties cost about 1000 more.\n- **Candidate 2:** it is also at about 1.1. Ordering every other day saves about 13 × 16 ≈ 208 in order fees. But it holds one day's demand of both items overnight every other day, which costs about 420 in storage, and large deliveries risk hitting the 176-unit limit.\n\n**Randomness.** Being 10 % above the forecast is almost free when demand comes in high but costs a lot when it comes in low. The open-loop plans can't correct, so excess just piles up. That makes the forecast plan the steadiest of the eight too."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4967, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2269, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4967}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp":